# The build context is the repo root, because one artifact needs both halves of the repo
# (frontend/ and backend/). That makes this file load-bearing rather than an optimisation: without
# it the context carries two virtualenvs, node_modules, and — the part that matters —
# `backend/.env`, straight into a layer nobody would think to look in.
#
# Deny-list rather than allow-list, matching .gitignore's shape so the two are readable side by
# side. `scripts/image-build.sh` prints the context size, which is the cheap way to notice this
# file has stopped working.

# --- secrets: the reason this file is not optional ---
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
.mcp.json
*.pem
*.key
secrets/

# --- git and CI ---
.git
.github
.gitignore

# --- Python: rebuilt inside the image, and a host venv would not run in it anyway ---
**/.venv
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
**/*.egg-info
**/.pytest_cache
**/.ruff_cache
**/.mypy_cache
**/.coverage
**/htmlcov

# --- Node: `npm ci` installs these in the spa stage ---
**/node_modules
# A stale local build must never reach a layer. The `spa` stage builds it from source, and the
# runtime stage copies it from there.
frontend/dist

# --- tests and docs: not shipped, and their absence keeps the runtime layer honest about what it
#     contains. The image is the product, not the workshop. ---
backend/tests
mcp/tests
kaya-client/tests
docs
**/*.md
# Each package's own pyproject.toml names its README as the project readme, and uv will not read
# the metadata without it — true of backend's image and, since KAN-573, of mcp's.
!backend/README.md
!mcp/README.md
!kaya-client/README.md

# --- deployment inputs, which describe the image and are not part of it ---
deploy
docker-compose.yml
Makefile
treehouse.toml

# --- editors / OS / agent tooling ---
.vscode
.idea
.claude
*.swp
.DS_Store
